{
  "title": "Chain of Thought Utilization in Large Language Models and Application in Nephrology - PMC",
  "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10819595",
  "snippet": "19.Mayo M. Unraveling the Power of Chain-of-Thought Prompting in Large Language Models. [(accessed on 13 November 2023)]. Available online: .\n 20.Ott S., Hebenstreit K., Lievin V., Hother C.E., Moradi M., Mayrhauser M., Praas R., Winther O., Samwald M. ThoughtSource: A central hub for large language model reasoning data. Sci. Data. 2023;10:528. doi: 10.1038/s41597-023-02433-3. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n 21.Ecoffet A. GPT-4 Technical Report. arXiv. 2023 doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2303.08774.2303.08774 [DOI] [Google Scholar]\n 22.Wolff T. How to Craft Prompts for Maximum Effectiveness. [(accessed on 14 November 2023)]. Available online: .\n 23.Ramlochan S. Master Prompting Concepts: Zero-Shot and Few-Shot Prompting. [(accessed on 25 April 2023)]. Available online: [...] Chain-of-thought prompting is a strategy employed to make LLMs work in a more focused, context-aware manner . Traditionally, LLMs work by predicting the next word in a sequence based on the context of the words that came before it. While they can handle quite complex queries and prompts, these models may lack the granularity to reason through intricate problems or questions, sometimes failing to maintain the semantic thread of the conversation or analysis. To overcome this limitation, chain-of-thought prompting breaks down a complex task or query into a sequence of simpler, interrelated prompts . Each prompt is designed to build upon the last, thereby guiding the LLM to think “step by step” through a problem. Essentially, you are creating a “chain” of thoughts that the model follows, much [...] 27.Pal A., Umapathi L.K., Sankarasubbu M. Med-HALT: Medical Domain Hallucination Test for Large Language Models. arXiv. 20232307.15343 [Google Scholar]\n 28.Wei J., Wang X., Schuurmans D., DBosma M., Ichter B., Xia F., Chi E., Le Q., Zhou D. Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models. arXiv. 20232201.11903 [Google Scholar]\n 29.Cobbe K., Kosaraju V., Bavarian M., Chen M., Jun H., Kaiser L., Plappert M., Tworek J., Hilton J., Nakano R., et al. Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems. arXiv. 20212110.14168 [Google Scholar]"
}